WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.327
Welcome to Week Four of CS104: Method of Proof, Part Two.

2
00:00:05.327 --> 00:00:09.685
This lecture extends our proof toolkit in three directions.

3
00:00:09.685 --> 00:00:15.496
We will split difficult claims into complete sets of cases, prove that

4
00:00:15.496 --> 00:00:20.338
mathematical objects exist, and prove that an object is unique.

5
00:00:20.338 --> 00:00:26.149
The central habit is to match the logical structure of a statement

6
00:00:26.149 --> 00:00:28.571
with the right proof strategy.

7
00:00:28.571 --> 00:00:34.382
As we work through each example, notice what must be assumed, what

8
00:00:34.382 --> 00:00:40.193
must be constructed, and what has to be shown in every branch.

9
00:00:40.193 --> 00:00:42.583
Here is our route.

10
00:00:42.583 --> 00:00:49.752
Section four point one develops proof by cases using parity, remainders, congruence,

11
00:00:49.752 --> 00:00:53.935
the pigeonhole principle, and the triangle inequality.

12
00:00:53.935 --> 00:01:01.104
Section four point two separates constructive existence proofs, where we exhibit a

13
00:01:01.104 --> 00:01:08.274
witness, from non-constructive proofs, where an argument guarantees a witness without locating

14
00:01:08.274 --> 00:01:09.469
it explicitly.

15
00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:16.639
Section four point three treats uniqueness as two obligations: existence and at-most-one.

16
00:01:16.639 --> 00:01:23.809
We finish by diagnosing common proof-writing errors and deciding how much justification

17
00:01:23.809 --> 00:01:26.199
a clear proof needs.

18
00:01:26.199 --> 00:01:29.322
We begin with proof by cases.

19
00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:35.569
This method is appropriate when every object in the domain belongs to

20
00:01:35.569 --> 00:01:38.172
one of several manageable categories.

21
00:01:38.172 --> 00:01:43.898
The cases must be exhaustive: together they must cover every possibility.

22
00:01:43.898 --> 00:01:50.145
They should also be chosen because the hypothesis or conclusion behaves more

23
00:01:50.145 --> 00:01:52.227
simply inside each branch.

24
00:01:52.227 --> 00:01:58.474
Once the desired conclusion has been proved in every possible case, it

25
00:01:58.474 --> 00:02:01.077
follows for the whole domain.

26
00:02:01.077 --> 00:02:06.610
The logical foundation is that an implication with a disjunctive hypothesis can

27
00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:08.916
be proved branch by branch.

28
00:02:08.916 --> 00:02:14.449
To establish open parenthesis p or q close parenthesis implies r, prove

29
00:02:14.449 --> 00:02:17.677
p implies r and q implies r.

30
00:02:17.677 --> 00:02:23.210
The divisibility proposition at the top works this way: if three divides

31
00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:28.744
m or three divides n, either branch makes the product m n

32
00:02:28.744 --> 00:02:30.588
a multiple of three.

33
00:02:30.588 --> 00:02:36.122
Proposition four point one point one has the same structure because every

34
00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:39.349
integer n is either even or odd.

35
00:02:39.349 --> 00:02:44.883
We will prove that n squared plus n is even in both

36
00:02:44.883 --> 00:02:45.344
branches.

37
00:02:45.344 --> 00:02:47.826
Let n be an arbitrary integer.

38
00:02:47.826 --> 00:02:51.550
If n is even, write n equals two k.

39
00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:56.515
Then n squared plus n equals four k squared plus two k,

40
00:02:56.515 --> 00:03:00.653
which factors as two k times two k plus one.

41
00:03:00.653 --> 00:03:04.376
This is twice an integer, so it is even.

42
00:03:04.376 --> 00:03:08.927
If n is odd, write n equals two k plus one.

43
00:03:08.927 --> 00:03:13.892
Expanding gives four k squared plus six k plus two, or two

44
00:03:13.892 --> 00:03:18.857
times open parenthesis two k squared plus three k plus one close

45
00:03:18.857 --> 00:03:19.271
parenthesis.

46
00:03:19.271 --> 00:03:20.926
That is also even.

47
00:03:20.926 --> 00:03:25.891
Because every integer is in exactly one of these cases, the proposition

48
00:03:25.891 --> 00:03:26.305
follows.

49
00:03:26.305 --> 00:03:32.464
Now consider proposition four point one point two: three divides n cubed

50
00:03:32.464 --> 00:03:35.544
minus n for every integer n.

51
00:03:35.544 --> 00:03:41.703
Splitting only into even and odd cases does not reveal divisibility by

52
00:03:41.703 --> 00:03:42.217
three.

53
00:03:42.217 --> 00:03:47.863
Parity is the wrong classification because it tracks remainders modulo two.

54
00:03:47.863 --> 00:03:54.022
Divisibility by three suggests three residue classes instead: remainder zero, one, or

55
00:03:54.022 --> 00:03:57.616
two when n is divided by three.

56
00:03:57.616 --> 00:04:03.262
Equivalently, n is congruent to zero, one, or two modulo three.

57
00:04:03.262 --> 00:04:08.908
A useful case split follows the arithmetic structure of the conclusion.

58
00:04:08.908 --> 00:04:12.203
We prove the proposition in three cases.

59
00:04:12.203 --> 00:04:17.850
If n equals three k, then n cubed minus n equals three

60
00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:22.557
times open parenthesis nine k cubed minus k close parenthesis.

61
00:04:22.557 --> 00:04:28.205
If n equals three k plus one, expansion gives three times open

62
00:04:28.205 --> 00:04:33.852
parenthesis nine k cubed plus nine k squared plus two k close

63
00:04:33.852 --> 00:04:34.323
parenthesis.

64
00:04:34.323 --> 00:04:39.971
If n equals three k plus two, expansion gives three times open

65
00:04:39.971 --> 00:04:45.619
parenthesis nine k cubed plus eighteen k squared plus eleven k plus

66
00:04:45.619 --> 00:04:47.030
two close parenthesis.

67
00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:51.737
In every branch the expression is three times an integer.

68
00:04:51.737 --> 00:04:57.385
The three possible remainders are exhaustive, so three divides n cubed minus

69
00:04:57.385 --> 00:04:59.738
n for every integer n.

70
00:04:59.738 --> 00:05:04.286
Parity creates two cases for one integer: even or odd.

71
00:05:04.286 --> 00:05:09.743
With two integers m and n, the complete parity split has four

72
00:05:09.743 --> 00:05:15.200
ordered cases: both even, both odd, m odd with n even, and

73
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.474
m even with n odd.

74
00:05:17.474 --> 00:05:22.477
Do not omit the mixed cases merely because they look similar.

75
00:05:22.477 --> 00:05:27.934
You may later combine symmetric branches, but first show that your grouping

76
00:05:27.934 --> 00:05:30.208
still covers every ordered pair.

77
00:05:30.208 --> 00:05:35.665
A case proof is only as strong as the completeness of its

78
00:05:35.665 --> 00:05:36.120
partition.

79
00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.197
Remainders give a systematic general partition.

80
00:05:39.197 --> 00:05:45.351
Modulo three, every integer is congruent to exactly one of zero, one,

81
00:05:45.351 --> 00:05:46.377
or two.

82
00:05:46.377 --> 00:05:52.531
Modulo a positive integer k, every integer is congruent to exactly one

83
00:05:52.531 --> 00:05:56.634
of zero, one, two, through k minus one.

84
00:05:56.634 --> 00:06:01.249
These are the possible remainders from the division algorithm.

85
00:06:01.249 --> 00:06:07.403
When a problem mentions divisibility by k, powers modulo k, or a

86
00:06:07.403 --> 00:06:13.557
repeating arithmetic pattern, these residue classes are often the natural cases to

87
00:06:13.557 --> 00:06:14.070
test.

88
00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.604
Congruence records equality of remainders.

89
00:06:16.604 --> 00:06:22.685
If r is the remainder when a is divided by positive n,

90
00:06:22.685 --> 00:06:26.738
then a is congruent to r modulo n.

91
00:06:26.738 --> 00:06:32.819
The integer a belongs to exactly one remainder class from zero through

92
00:06:32.819 --> 00:06:34.340
n minus one.

93
00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:40.420
Two integers a and b are congruent modulo n exactly when division

94
00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:43.461
by n leaves the same remainder.

95
00:06:43.461 --> 00:06:46.501
Equivalently, n divides a minus b.

96
00:06:46.501 --> 00:06:52.582
Congruence therefore lets us reason about a whole class of integers without

97
00:06:52.582 --> 00:06:56.129
repeatedly writing the quotient and remainder equation.

98
00:06:56.129 --> 00:07:02.280
Proposition four point one point four is an application of the pigeonhole

99
00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:02.793
principle.

100
00:07:02.793 --> 00:07:05.868
Divide five distinct integers by four.

101
00:07:05.868 --> 00:07:11.507
There are only four possible remainders: zero, one, two, and three.

102
00:07:11.507 --> 00:07:17.658
Think of the integers as five pigeons and the remainder classes as

103
00:07:17.658 --> 00:07:18.683
four pigeonholes.

104
00:07:18.683 --> 00:07:24.834
Since five objects are assigned to only four classes, at least two

105
00:07:24.834 --> 00:07:27.397
must occupy the same class.

106
00:07:27.397 --> 00:07:33.548
Those two integers have the same remainder and are therefore congruent modulo

107
00:07:33.548 --> 00:07:34.060
four.

108
00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:40.211
Distinctness is not needed for the counting step, but it ensures we

109
00:07:40.211 --> 00:07:43.799
have two different integers sharing the class.

110
00:07:43.799 --> 00:07:46.607
Case splits also arise from order.

111
00:07:46.607 --> 00:07:50.818
Every real number x is positive, zero, or negative.

112
00:07:50.818 --> 00:07:56.432
Depending on the expression, these three cases can be combined into two

113
00:07:56.432 --> 00:08:02.047
larger but still exhaustive branches: x greater than or equal to zero

114
00:08:02.047 --> 00:08:07.661
versus x less than zero, or x equal to zero versus x

115
00:08:07.661 --> 00:08:09.533
not equal to zero.

116
00:08:09.533 --> 00:08:14.679
The correct grouping depends on which definition changes at the boundary.

117
00:08:14.679 --> 00:08:20.294
Always verify that the combined cases cover the full domain and do

118
00:08:20.294 --> 00:08:22.165
not leave a gap.

119
00:08:22.165 --> 00:08:27.653
For two real numbers a and b, equality gives the two-case split

120
00:08:27.653 --> 00:08:31.769
a equals b or a does not equal b.

121
00:08:31.769 --> 00:08:37.257
Order gives the three-case split a greater than b, a equals b,

122
00:08:37.257 --> 00:08:39.544
or a less than b.

123
00:08:39.544 --> 00:08:43.202
These alternatives support many uniqueness and inequality arguments.

124
00:08:43.202 --> 00:08:48.690
A proof may sometimes combine greater than and less than into a

125
00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.891
single case a not equal to b.

126
00:08:51.891 --> 00:08:57.379
Again, the best partition is the one that exposes the definition or

127
00:08:57.379 --> 00:08:59.209
operation controlling the claim.

128
00:08:59.209 --> 00:09:04.370
The triangle inequality states that the absolute value of x plus y

129
00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:09.531
is at most the absolute value of x plus the absolute value

130
00:09:09.531 --> 00:09:10.392
of y.

131
00:09:10.392 --> 00:09:15.553
Absolute value itself is defined by cases: absolute x equals x when

132
00:09:15.553 --> 00:09:19.854
x is nonnegative and negative x when x is negative.

133
00:09:19.854 --> 00:09:25.015
Because the formula changes with the sign, a case proof is natural.

134
00:09:25.015 --> 00:09:30.177
We must account not only for the signs of x and y,

135
00:09:30.177 --> 00:09:34.908
but in mixed-sign cases also for the sign of their sum.

136
00:09:34.908 --> 00:09:39.810
In the first case, x and y are both nonnegative.

137
00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:42.262
Their sum is also nonnegative.

138
00:09:42.262 --> 00:09:48.145
The definition therefore gives absolute x equals x, absolute y equals y,

139
00:09:48.145 --> 00:09:52.557
and absolute x plus y equals x plus y.

140
00:09:52.557 --> 00:09:58.440
The two sides of the triangle inequality are actually equal in this

141
00:09:58.440 --> 00:09:58.930
branch.

142
00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:04.813
Equality is stronger than the required less-than-or-equal conclusion, so the case is

143
00:10:04.813 --> 00:10:05.303
complete.

144
00:10:05.303 --> 00:10:08.052
Now take x nonnegative and y negative.

145
00:10:08.052 --> 00:10:12.371
Then absolute x equals x and absolute y equals negative y.

146
00:10:12.371 --> 00:10:15.513
We split again according to x plus y.

147
00:10:15.513 --> 00:10:20.224
If the sum is nonnegative, absolute x plus y equals x plus

148
00:10:20.224 --> 00:10:24.936
y, and y is at most negative y, so x plus y

149
00:10:24.936 --> 00:10:27.292
is at most x minus y.

150
00:10:27.292 --> 00:10:32.004
If the sum is negative, absolute x plus y equals negative x

151
00:10:32.004 --> 00:10:32.790
minus y.

152
00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:37.501
Since x is nonnegative, negative x is at most x, so negative

153
00:10:37.501 --> 00:10:41.035
x minus y is at most x minus y.

154
00:10:41.035 --> 00:10:45.747
In both subcases, absolute x plus y is at most absolute x

155
00:10:45.747 --> 00:10:46.925
plus absolute y.

156
00:10:46.925 --> 00:10:52.866
The third case, x negative and y nonnegative, is symmetric to the

157
00:10:52.866 --> 00:10:55.342
second after interchanging the variables.

158
00:10:55.342 --> 00:11:01.282
In the fourth case, both numbers are negative, so their sum is

159
00:11:01.282 --> 00:11:01.778
negative.

160
00:11:01.778 --> 00:11:07.718
Then absolute x plus y equals negative x minus y, while absolute

161
00:11:07.718 --> 00:11:13.659
x plus absolute y also equals negative x minus y; equality holds.

162
00:11:13.659 --> 00:11:19.600
We have covered all four sign combinations, including the necessary mixed-sign subcases,

163
00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:25.046
so the triangle inequality holds for all real x and y.

164
00:11:25.046 --> 00:11:29.220
Section four point two turns to existence statements.

165
00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:35.480
Instead of proving a property for every object, we must establish that

166
00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:40.176
at least one object satisfying specified conditions is present.

167
00:11:40.176 --> 00:11:46.437
The central question becomes: can we exhibit a witness directly, or must

168
00:11:46.437 --> 00:11:51.132
we prove indirectly that a witness has to exist?

169
00:11:51.132 --> 00:11:53.453
Existence statements involve existential quantifiers.

170
00:11:53.453 --> 00:11:59.024
The simple form says there exists x such that p of x.

171
00:11:59.024 --> 00:12:04.595
A mixed form such as there exists x for every y, p

172
00:12:04.595 --> 00:12:10.166
of x and y, asks for one x that works for all

173
00:12:10.166 --> 00:12:10.630
y.

174
00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:16.201
By contrast, for every x there exists y, p of x and

175
00:12:16.201 --> 00:12:20.379
y, allows the chosen y to depend on x.

176
00:12:20.379 --> 00:12:21.772
Quantifier order matters.

177
00:12:21.772 --> 00:12:27.343
Before proving existence, identify the domain, the conditions on the witness, and

178
00:12:27.343 --> 00:12:32.914
whether one witness must work uniformly or may vary with earlier choices.

179
00:12:32.914 --> 00:12:35.732
There are two broad approaches.

180
00:12:35.732 --> 00:12:42.496
A constructive proof gives a specific object and verifies that it satisfies

181
00:12:42.496 --> 00:12:44.187
every required condition.

182
00:12:44.187 --> 00:12:50.951
A non-constructive proof gives an argument that some object must exist even

183
00:12:50.951 --> 00:12:54.333
when no convenient example is produced.

184
00:12:54.333 --> 00:12:57.151
Neither method is automatically superior.

185
00:12:57.151 --> 00:13:02.224
The statement and available theory determine the useful approach.

186
00:13:02.224 --> 00:13:08.988
In both cases, the proof must address every condition in the existential

187
00:13:08.988 --> 00:13:09.552
claim.

188
00:13:09.552 --> 00:13:15.121
Proposition four point two point one asks for nonzero real numbers x

189
00:13:15.121 --> 00:13:18.370
and y whose squares sum to one.

190
00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:23.939
A constructive proof chooses x equals one over square root of two

191
00:13:23.939 --> 00:13:27.188
and y equal to the same value.

192
00:13:27.188 --> 00:13:29.509
Both are real and nonzero.

193
00:13:29.509 --> 00:13:34.150
Squaring each gives one half, so their sum is one.

194
00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:39.255
The witness alone is not the proof; the verification is essential.

195
00:13:39.255 --> 00:13:44.824
We have checked the domain, the nonzero condition, and the equation, so

196
00:13:44.824 --> 00:13:46.681
the existence claim follows.

197
00:13:46.681 --> 00:13:52.318
Proposition four point two point two states that x to the fourth

198
00:13:52.318 --> 00:13:57.486
minus three x plus one equals zero has a real solution.

199
00:13:57.486 --> 00:14:00.775
Let f of x equal that polynomial.

200
00:14:00.775 --> 00:14:02.654
Polynomial functions are continuous.

201
00:14:02.654 --> 00:14:08.292
We calculate f of zero equals one and f of one equals

202
00:14:08.292 --> 00:14:09.232
negative one.

203
00:14:09.232 --> 00:14:14.870
Since the function changes sign between zero and one, the intermediate value

204
00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:20.507
theorem guarantees some c strictly between zero and one with f of

205
00:14:20.507 --> 00:14:22.387
c equal to zero.

206
00:14:22.387 --> 00:14:28.024
This is non-constructive: it proves a root exists without telling us its

207
00:14:28.024 --> 00:14:28.964
exact value.

208
00:14:28.964 --> 00:14:34.968
Here the statement contains an existential choice followed by a universal condition.

209
00:14:34.968 --> 00:14:39.471
Choose x equal to one thousand factorial plus one.

210
00:14:39.471 --> 00:14:42.974
This integer is greater than one thousand.

211
00:14:42.974 --> 00:14:48.978
For any integer n with one less than n less than one

212
00:14:48.978 --> 00:14:51.980
thousand, n divides one thousand factorial.

213
00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:57.984
If n also divided one thousand factorial plus one, it would divide

214
00:14:57.984 --> 00:15:00.486
their difference, which is one.

215
00:15:00.486 --> 00:15:04.489
But no integer greater than one divides one.

216
00:15:04.489 --> 00:15:10.493
Therefore our chosen x is not divisible by any permitted n, completing

217
00:15:10.493 --> 00:15:11.994
the constructive proof.

218
00:15:11.994 --> 00:15:16.944
Let A be the average of real numbers s one through s

219
00:15:16.944 --> 00:15:17.357
n.

220
00:15:17.357 --> 00:15:21.895
We prove that at least one entry is at least A.

221
00:15:21.895 --> 00:15:26.433
Suppose the opposite: every s i is strictly less than A.

222
00:15:26.433 --> 00:15:31.383
Adding all n inequalities gives the sum strictly less than n A.

223
00:15:31.383 --> 00:15:36.334
Since n is positive, division by n gives the average strictly less

224
00:15:36.334 --> 00:15:37.159
than A.

225
00:15:37.159 --> 00:15:40.047
But the average is A by definition.

226
00:15:40.047 --> 00:15:44.997
This contradiction shows that not every entry can be below the average,

227
00:15:44.997 --> 00:15:49.947
so at least one s i is greater than or equal to

228
00:15:49.947 --> 00:15:50.360
A.

229
00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:54.518
Section four point three strengthens existence to uniqueness.

230
00:15:54.518 --> 00:16:00.754
A uniqueness statement says there is exactly one object with a property.

231
00:16:00.754 --> 00:16:06.991
Such a proof has two separate obligations: show that at least one

232
00:16:06.991 --> 00:16:13.228
object exists, and show that no two distinct objects can both satisfy

233
00:16:13.228 --> 00:16:14.267
the property.

234
00:16:14.267 --> 00:16:20.107
Keywords such as exactly one, unique, and only one signal a uniqueness

235
00:16:20.107 --> 00:16:20.594
claim.

236
00:16:20.594 --> 00:16:26.434
The equation x cubed plus one equals zero has the unique real

237
00:16:26.434 --> 00:16:27.894
solution negative one.

238
00:16:27.894 --> 00:16:33.734
A fixed-point equation for cosine has exactly one solution in the stated

239
00:16:33.734 --> 00:16:34.220
interval.

240
00:16:34.220 --> 00:16:40.060
The number seven is the only prime of the form n cubed

241
00:16:40.060 --> 00:16:41.034
minus one.

242
00:16:41.034 --> 00:16:43.467
Each claim needs both parts.

243
00:16:43.467 --> 00:16:49.307
Finding one example proves existence but not uniqueness; ruling out two different

244
00:16:49.307 --> 00:16:55.147
examples proves at most one but does not prove that any example

245
00:16:55.147 --> 00:16:55.633
exists.

246
00:16:55.633 --> 00:17:00.932
The symbol there exists exactly one x such that p of x

247
00:17:00.932 --> 00:17:02.256
packages two statements.

248
00:17:02.256 --> 00:17:07.113
The existence part is there exists x with p of x.

249
00:17:07.113 --> 00:17:12.411
The uniqueness part says that for all a and b, if p

250
00:17:12.411 --> 00:17:16.826
of a and p of b, then a equals b.

251
00:17:16.826 --> 00:17:20.800
This second condition is often called at most one.

252
00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:26.098
A complete uniqueness proof labels these two parts so the reader can

253
00:17:26.098 --> 00:17:29.189
see that neither obligation has been omitted.

254
00:17:29.189 --> 00:17:33.712
There are several ways to prove the uniqueness part.

255
00:17:33.712 --> 00:17:39.742
Directly, assume a and b both satisfy the property and derive a

256
00:17:39.742 --> 00:17:40.747
equals b.

257
00:17:40.747 --> 00:17:46.778
By contrapositive, assume a differs from b and show that at least

258
00:17:46.778 --> 00:17:49.291
one cannot satisfy the property.

259
00:17:49.291 --> 00:17:55.321
By contradiction, assume a and b are distinct and both satisfy the

260
00:17:55.321 --> 00:17:57.834
property, then derive an impossibility.

261
00:17:57.834 --> 00:18:01.352
All three establish the same at-most-one condition.

262
00:18:01.352 --> 00:18:07.383
Choose the form that makes the defining equation or structure easiest to

263
00:18:07.383 --> 00:18:07.885
use.

264
00:18:07.885 --> 00:18:13.535
Proposition four point three point three says x cubed plus one equals

265
00:18:13.535 --> 00:18:16.360
zero has a unique real solution.

266
00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:19.656
For existence, substitute x equals negative one.

267
00:18:19.656 --> 00:18:23.893
For uniqueness, let a and b be real solutions.

268
00:18:23.893 --> 00:18:29.543
Then a cubed equals b cubed, so open parenthesis a minus b

269
00:18:29.543 --> 00:18:35.192
close parenthesis times open parenthesis a squared plus a b plus b

270
00:18:35.192 --> 00:18:37.547
squared close parenthesis equals zero.

271
00:18:37.547 --> 00:18:42.255
Both roots are negative, so the second factor is positive.

272
00:18:42.255 --> 00:18:44.138
It cannot be zero.

273
00:18:44.138 --> 00:18:48.846
Therefore a minus b equals zero and a equals b.

274
00:18:48.846 --> 00:18:55.268
A prime number is a positive integer greater than one whose only

275
00:18:55.268 --> 00:18:58.478
positive divisors are one and itself.

276
00:18:58.478 --> 00:19:04.900
A composite number is a positive integer greater than one that is

277
00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:05.970
not prime.

278
00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:10.251
The number one is neither prime nor composite.

279
00:19:10.251 --> 00:19:16.673
These definitions matter because a factorisation proves compositeness only when it expresses

280
00:19:16.673 --> 00:19:23.095
the number as a product of positive integers both greater than one.

281
00:19:23.095 --> 00:19:28.376
Proposition four point three point four says the only prime number of

282
00:19:28.376 --> 00:19:31.896
the form n cubed minus one is seven.

283
00:19:31.896 --> 00:19:34.977
Existence is immediate at n equals two.

284
00:19:34.977 --> 00:19:40.258
For uniqueness, factor n cubed minus one as n minus one times

285
00:19:40.258 --> 00:19:42.899
n squared plus n plus one.

286
00:19:42.899 --> 00:19:48.180
Values n less than or equal to one do not produce a

287
00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:49.060
positive prime.

288
00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:54.341
If n is greater than two, both factors are integers greater than

289
00:19:54.341 --> 00:19:56.982
one, so the product is composite.

290
00:19:56.982 --> 00:20:01.823
The only remaining case is n equals two, which produces seven.

291
00:20:01.823 --> 00:20:05.783
Thus seven is the unique prime of this form.

292
00:20:05.783 --> 00:20:12.232
This summary connects a statement’s quantifier with the role of examples.

293
00:20:12.232 --> 00:20:19.267
A supporting example does not prove a universal statement; after seeing one

294
00:20:19.267 --> 00:20:25.715
successful case, we must still prove the property for every case.

295
00:20:25.715 --> 00:20:30.405
A violating example immediately disproves a universal statement.

296
00:20:30.405 --> 00:20:37.440
For an existential statement, one supporting example proves the claim, while a

297
00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:42.130
failed candidate proves nothing about other possible witnesses.

298
00:20:42.130 --> 00:20:48.579
The logical form tells us what evidence can settle the question.

299
00:20:48.579 --> 00:20:51.762
The comic illustrates a circular argument.

300
00:20:51.762 --> 00:20:58.128
“Clean the mess because it needs to be cleaned” merely restates the

301
00:20:58.128 --> 00:21:00.781
conclusion as its own reason.

302
00:21:00.781 --> 00:21:07.148
In a mathematical proof, the conclusion cannot be used as an unsupported

303
00:21:07.148 --> 00:21:07.678
premise.

304
00:21:07.678 --> 00:21:14.045
The chain of reasoning must begin with the hypothesis, definitions, axioms, or

305
00:21:14.045 --> 00:21:19.881
earlier results and then supply an independent bridge to the claim.

306
00:21:19.881 --> 00:21:22.649
Common proof errors are structural.

307
00:21:22.649 --> 00:21:27.633
Misinterpret the statement, and you prove the wrong claim.

308
00:21:27.633 --> 00:21:34.278
Use examples to prove a universal statement, and you have checked only

309
00:21:34.278 --> 00:21:35.385
special cases.

310
00:21:35.385 --> 00:21:41.476
Reuse one symbol for different objects, and the logic becomes ambiguous.

311
00:21:41.476 --> 00:21:48.121
Prove a converse instead of the stated implication, and the direction is

312
00:21:48.121 --> 00:21:48.675
wrong.

313
00:21:48.675 --> 00:21:53.658
Jump to a conclusion, and essential justification is missing.

314
00:21:53.658 --> 00:21:58.642
Beg the question, and the conclusion has been assumed.

315
00:21:58.642 --> 00:22:05.287
A reliable final check is to mark the hypothesis, target, domain, witnesses,

316
00:22:05.287 --> 00:22:08.609
case coverage, and every cited result.

317
00:22:08.609 --> 00:22:13.228
A proof should be complete without becoming an essay.

318
00:22:13.228 --> 00:22:19.387
Obvious arithmetic facts may need no detailed derivation, but any step that

319
00:22:19.387 --> 00:22:22.980
carries the logical burden should be justified.

320
00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:26.059
State the method you are using.

321
00:22:26.059 --> 00:22:28.625
Define what each symbol represents.

322
00:22:28.625 --> 00:22:34.784
Cite earlier results clearly, even when you do not repeat their proofs.

323
00:22:34.784 --> 00:22:39.916
Use enough words to explain why each equation is relevant.

324
00:22:39.916 --> 00:22:46.075
The goal is not maximum length; it is a readable argument that

325
00:22:46.075 --> 00:22:49.154
another student can verify without guessing.

326
00:22:49.154 --> 00:22:52.617
Let us bring Week Four together.

327
00:22:52.617 --> 00:22:59.544
Use proof by cases when an exhaustive partition makes each branch manageable.

328
00:22:59.544 --> 00:23:06.470
Let divisibility guide you toward remainder classes, and verify that no case

329
00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:07.624
is missing.

330
00:23:07.624 --> 00:23:14.551
For existence, either construct and check a witness or use a theorem

331
00:23:14.551 --> 00:23:17.437
or contradiction that guarantees one.

332
00:23:17.437 --> 00:23:21.477
For uniqueness, prove both existence and at-most-one.

333
00:23:21.477 --> 00:23:28.403
Examples have different force under universal and existential quantifiers, so read the

334
00:23:28.403 --> 00:23:30.712
statement before choosing evidence.

335
00:23:30.712 --> 00:23:37.638
Complete the mastery check now, then practise labelling the method, assumptions, witnesses,

336
00:23:37.638 --> 00:23:42.020
cases, and final conclusion in your own proofs.
